# The kings and princes of Wales, batch 4 (November 2026): the princes of Powys and of Deheubarth after the Lord Rhys.
# Owain Cyfeiliog (longer page), and new pages for Gwenwynwyn, Madog ap Gruffudd Maelor, Maelgwn ap Rhys and Rhys Gryg.
# Same format and house rules as src/princes.py, which adds LONG, PEOPLE and CITES from here to its own.
# Sources checked (November 2026): the Dictionary of Welsh Biography (Owain Cyfeiliog, Maelgwn ap Rhys, Rhys Gryg);
# Gerald of Wales, Itinerary through Wales, Book II chapter 12; Coflein (RCAHMW) on the battle of Painscastle, 1198;
# Brut y Tywysogion (tr. Thomas Jones); Wikipedia on each prince.
# Death years follow the Dictionary of Welsh Biography: Maelgwn late in 1230, Rhys Gryg 1234.
from people2a import P

LONG = {}

LONG["owain-cyfeiliog"] = (
 ["Owain Cyfeiliog was a prince of Powys and one of the finest poets of medieval Wales. For thirty-five years he ruled southern Powys, the land that his son Gwenwynwyn would give his name to, and he ruled it so well that Gerald of Wales counted him among the three Welsh princes most famous for justice, wisdom and moderation. He founded the abbey of Ystrad Marchell, and the poem Hirlas Owain, the drinking-horn of Owain, is the finest picture we have of a Welsh prince and his war band.",
  "|| Born | about 1130",
  "|| Father | Gruffudd ap Maredudd, of the royal house of Powys",
  "|| Lord of Cyfeiliog | from 1149, under his uncle Madog ap Maredudd",
  "|| Prince of southern Powys | 1160–1195",
  "|| Founded | the abbey of Ystrad Marchell (Strata Marcella), 1170",
  "|| Poem | Hirlas Owain",
  "|| Died | 1197, a monk at Ystrad Marchell, and buried there",
  "## Prince of Powys",
  "Owain was the son of Gruffudd ap Maredudd and the nephew of Madog ap Maredudd, the last king of all Powys. In 1149 Madog made him lord of the cantref of Cyfeiliog, in the hills around Machynlleth, and the name stayed with him for life. When Madog died in 1160, Powys was shared among his heirs, and Owain took the greater part of the south. In 1165, when Henry II marched on Wales with a great army, Owain stood with the princes of Wales under Owain Gwynedd in the Berwyn, and the English king was driven home by the Welsh and the Welsh weather.[[cite:dwb-owain-cyfeiliog,wiki-owain-cyfeiliog]]",
  "## The eloquent prince",
  "Gerald of Wales, who came through Powys in 1188, wrote that Owain spoke more fluently than any prince of his day and governed his land well. He tells a story of him at Henry II’s table at Shrewsbury. The king sent him a loaf as a mark of honour. Owain broke it into small pieces and set them aside, and ate them one by one, like an almoner sharing out alms. When the king asked why, Owain answered that he was following the example of his lord, for Henry was known for keeping the wealth of vacant churches in his own hands.[[cite:gerald-itin-owain,dwb-owain-cyfeiliog]]",
  "> Owen was a man of more fluent speech than his contemporary princes, and was conspicuous for the good management of his territory | Gerald of Wales, Itinerary through Wales, 1191",
  "## Hirlas Owain",
  "Owain was a poet as well as a prince. The poem Hirlas Owain is set in his hall after a victory, a raid of 1155 to free his brother Meurig from prison in Maelor. The prince bids the cupbearer fill the long blue drinking horn and carry it to each of his warriors in turn, and he praises each man for his courage in the fight, and mourns those who did not come home. It follows the pattern of the Gododdin, and it is the best picture we have of the life of a Welsh prince and his war band on campaign. Scholars still debate whether Owain wrote it alone or with his court poet Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr, who also sang his praise.[[cite:dwb-owain-cyfeiliog,wiki-owain-cyfeiliog]]",
  "## Ystrad Marchell",
  "In 1170 Owain gave land by the Severn near Welshpool for a house of Cistercian monks, the abbey of Ystrad Marchell, Strata Marcella. It became one of the great Welsh abbeys, a home of learning and of the Welsh language. About 1195 Owain handed his lands to his son Gwenwynwyn and went to live among the monks there himself. He died at Ystrad Marchell in 1197 and was buried in the abbey he had founded.[[cite:dwb-owain-cyfeiliog,wiki-owain-cyfeiliog]]"],
 ["Tywysog Powys ac un o feirdd gorau Cymru’r Oesoedd Canol oedd Owain Cyfeiliog. Am dri deg pump o flynyddoedd rheolodd dde Powys, y wlad y rhoddai ei fab Gwenwynwyn ei enw iddi, a’i rheoli cystal nes i Gerallt Gymro ei gyfrif ymhlith y tri thywysog Cymreig enwocaf am gyfiawnder, doethineb a chymedroldeb. Sefydlodd abaty Ystrad Marchell, a’r gerdd Hirlas Owain yw’r darlun gorau sydd gennym o dywysog Cymreig a’i osgordd.",
  "|| Ganed | tua 1130",
  "|| Tad | Gruffudd ap Maredudd, o dŷ brenhinol Powys",
  "|| Arglwydd Cyfeiliog | o 1149, dan ei ewythr Madog ap Maredudd",
  "|| Tywysog de Powys | 1160–1195",
  "|| Sefydlodd | abaty Ystrad Marchell, 1170",
  "|| Cerdd | Hirlas Owain",
  "|| Bu farw | 1197, yn fynach yn Ystrad Marchell, a’i gladdu yno",
  "## Tywysog Powys",
  "Mab Gruffudd ap Maredudd a nai Madog ap Maredudd, brenin olaf Powys gyfan, oedd Owain. Yn 1149 gwnaeth Madog ef yn arglwydd cantref Cyfeiliog, yn y bryniau o gwmpas Machynlleth, a glynodd yr enw wrtho am ei oes. Pan fu farw Madog yn 1160, rhannwyd Powys rhwng ei etifeddion, a chymerodd Owain y rhan fwyaf o’r de. Yn 1165, pan orymdeithiodd Harri II ar Gymru gyda byddin fawr, safodd Owain gyda thywysogion Cymru dan Owain Gwynedd yn y Berwyn, a gyrrwyd brenin Lloegr adref gan y Cymry a thywydd Cymru.[[cite:dwb-owain-cyfeiliog,wiki-owain-cyfeiliog]]",
  "## Y tywysog huawdl",
  "Ysgrifennodd Gerallt Gymro, a ddaeth drwy Bowys yn 1188, fod Owain yn siarad yn rhwyddach nag unrhyw dywysog yn ei ddydd a’i fod yn llywodraethu ei wlad yn dda. Mae’n adrodd stori amdano wrth fwrdd Harri II yn Amwythig. Anfonodd y brenin dorth ato yn arwydd o anrhydedd. Torrodd Owain hi’n ddarnau mân a’u gosod o’r neilltu, a’u bwyta fesul un, fel elusennwr yn rhannu elusen. Pan ofynnodd y brenin pam, atebodd Owain ei fod yn dilyn esiampl ei arglwydd, oherwydd roedd Harri’n enwog am gadw cyfoeth eglwysi gwag yn ei ddwylo ei hun.[[cite:gerald-itin-owain,dwb-owain-cyfeiliog]]",
  "> roedd Owain yn ŵr huotlach ei ymadrodd na thywysogion ei oes, ac yn amlwg am lywodraethu ei diriogaeth yn dda | Gerallt Gymro, Hanes y Daith trwy Gymru, 1191",
  "## Hirlas Owain",
  "Roedd Owain yn fardd yn ogystal â thywysog. Lleolir y gerdd Hirlas Owain yn ei neuadd wedi buddugoliaeth, cyrch yn 1155 i ryddhau ei frawd Meurig o garchar ym Maelor. Mae’r tywysog yn gorchymyn i’r trulliad lenwi’r corn yfed hir glas a’i gludo at bob un o’i ryfelwyr yn ei dro, ac mae’n canmol pob gŵr am ei ddewrder yn y frwydr, ac yn galaru am y rhai na ddaeth adref. Mae’n dilyn patrwm y Gododdin, a dyma’r darlun gorau sydd gennym o fywyd tywysog Cymreig a’i osgordd ar ymgyrch. Mae ysgolheigion yn dal i drafod ai Owain a’i canodd ar ei ben ei hun neu gyda’i fardd llys Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr, a ganodd ei glod yntau.[[cite:dwb-owain-cyfeiliog,wiki-owain-cyfeiliog]]",
  "## Ystrad Marchell",
  "Yn 1170 rhoddodd Owain dir ar lan Hafren ger y Trallwng i dŷ o fynaich Sistersaidd, abaty Ystrad Marchell. Daeth yn un o abatai mawr Cymru, yn gartref i ddysg ac i’r iaith Gymraeg. Tua 1195 trosglwyddodd Owain ei diroedd i’w fab Gwenwynwyn ac aeth i fyw ymhlith y mynaich yno ei hun. Bu farw yn Ystrad Marchell yn 1197 a’i gladdu yn yr abaty a sefydlodd.[[cite:dwb-owain-cyfeiliog,wiki-owain-cyfeiliog]]"],
)

PEOPLE = [
 P("gwenwynwyn", "medieval", "d. 1216", "Gwenwynwyn ab Owain", "Gwenwynwyn ab Owain", "Prince of Powys who hoped to lead all Wales", "Tywysog Powys a obeithiai arwain Cymru gyfan", "Gwenwynwyn_ab_Owain", "rulers",
   "Son of Owain Cyfeiliog and prince of southern Powys from 1195, which has been called Powys Wenwynwyn after him ever since. In 1198 he raised the Welsh to win back their ancient bounds.",
   "Mab Owain Cyfeiliog a thywysog de Powys o 1195, a elwir yn Bowys Wenwynwyn ar ei ôl byth oddi ar hynny. Yn 1198 cododd y Cymry i ennill eu hen derfynau yn ôl.",
   ["Gwenwynwyn ab Owain was the son of the poet-prince Owain Cyfeiliog, and he ruled southern Powys from 1195 until 1216. His name stayed on the land: southern Powys has been called Powys Wenwynwyn ever since. For a few years at the end of the twelfth century he was the strongest prince in Wales, and he set out to lead the whole nation.",
    "|| Father | Owain Cyfeiliog",
    "|| Prince of southern Powys | 1195–1216",
    "|| Wife | Margaret Corbet, daughter of the lord of Caus",
    "|| Great campaign | Painscastle, 1198",
    "|| Son | Gruffudd ap Gwenwynwyn",
    "|| Died | 1216",
    "## Painscastle, 1198",
    "When the Lord Rhys died in 1197, Gwenwynwyn stepped forward as the leader of the Welsh. The Welsh chronicle says that he wished to win back for the Welsh their ancient rights and their ancient bounds. In the summer of 1198 he gathered a great army from all over Wales and marched on Painscastle in Elfael, the castle of William de Braose, and laid siege to it for three weeks. On 13 October an English army under Geoffrey fitz Peter came to relieve it, and the Welsh were beaten with heavy loss. Among the fallen were Anarawd ab Einion, Owain Cascob ap Cadwallon, Rhiryd ap Iestyn and Robert ap Hywel.[[cite:coflein-painscastle,brut-jones,wiki-gwenwynwyn]]",
    "## Powys Wenwynwyn",
    "Gwenwynwyn made peace with King John in 1199 and married Margaret Corbet, the daughter of the lord of Caus. But his strength made the English crown wary, and in 1208 John seized him at Shrewsbury and took his lands. They were given back after two years. From 1212 Gwenwynwyn stood with Llywelyn Fawr and the other princes of Wales against John.[[cite:wiki-gwenwynwyn]]",
    "In 1216 the alliance broke, Llywelyn drove him out of Powys, and Gwenwynwyn took refuge in Chester. He died that same year. His son Gruffudd ap Gwenwynwyn won back the land after Llywelyn’s death, and the line of Gwenwynwyn held Powys Wenwynwyn for generations to come.[[cite:wiki-gwenwynwyn]]"],
   ["Mab y tywysog-fardd Owain Cyfeiliog oedd Gwenwynwyn ab Owain, a rheolodd dde Powys o 1195 hyd 1216. Arhosodd ei enw ar y wlad: Powys Wenwynwyn y gelwir de Powys byth oddi ar hynny. Am rai blynyddoedd ar ddiwedd y ddeuddegfed ganrif ef oedd y tywysog cryfaf yng Nghymru, a mynnodd arwain y genedl gyfan.",
    "|| Tad | Owain Cyfeiliog",
    "|| Tywysog de Powys | 1195–1216",
    "|| Gwraig | Margaret Corbet, merch arglwydd Cawrse",
    "|| Ymgyrch fawr | Castell Paen, 1198",
    "|| Mab | Gruffudd ap Gwenwynwyn",
    "|| Bu farw | 1216",
    "## Castell Paen, 1198",
    "Pan fu farw’r Arglwydd Rhys yn 1197, camodd Gwenwynwyn ymlaen yn arweinydd y Cymry. Dywed y cronicl Cymraeg ei fod am ennill yn ôl i’r Cymry eu hen hawliau a’u hen derfynau. Yn haf 1198 casglodd fyddin fawr o bob rhan o Gymru a gorymdeithio ar Gastell Paen yn Elfael, castell Gwilym Brewys, a gwarchae arno am dair wythnos. Ar 13 Hydref daeth byddin Seisnig dan Geoffrey fitz Peter i’w achub, a threchwyd y Cymry gyda cholled drom. Ymhlith y rhai a syrthiodd roedd Anarawd ab Einion, Owain Cascob ap Cadwallon, Rhiryd ap Iestyn a Robert ap Hywel.[[cite:coflein-painscastle,brut-jones,wiki-gwenwynwyn]]",
    "## Powys Wenwynwyn",
    "Gwnaeth Gwenwynwyn heddwch â’r Brenin John yn 1199 a phriodi Margaret Corbet, merch arglwydd Cawrse. Ond roedd ei nerth yn peri i goron Lloegr fod yn wyliadwrus, ac yn 1208 daliodd John ef yn Amwythig a chymryd ei diroedd. Fe’u rhoddwyd yn ôl wedi dwy flynedd. O 1212 safodd Gwenwynwyn gyda Llywelyn Fawr a thywysogion eraill Cymru yn erbyn John.[[cite:wiki-gwenwynwyn]]",
    "Yn 1216 torrodd y gynghrair, gyrrodd Llywelyn ef allan o Bowys, a llochesodd Gwenwynwyn yng Nghaer. Bu farw’r un flwyddyn. Enillodd ei fab Gruffudd ap Gwenwynwyn y wlad yn ôl wedi marw Llywelyn, a daliodd llinach Gwenwynwyn Bowys Wenwynwyn am genedlaethau i ddod.[[cite:wiki-gwenwynwyn]]"]),
 P("madog-ap-gruffudd-maelor", "medieval", "d. 1236", "Madog ap Gruffudd Maelor", "Madog ap Gruffudd Maelor", "Prince of northern Powys, founder of Valle Crucis", "Tywysog gogledd Powys, sylfaenydd Glyn y Groes", "Madog_ap_Gruffydd_Maelor", "rulers",
   "Prince of northern Powys, Powys Fadog, from 1191 to 1236, grandson of Owain Gwynedd and cousin of Llywelyn Fawr. In 1201 he founded the abbey of Glyn y Groes, Valle Crucis, near Llangollen, where he lies buried.",
   "Tywysog gogledd Powys, Powys Fadog, o 1191 hyd 1236, ŵyr i Owain Gwynedd a chefnder i Lywelyn Fawr. Yn 1201 sefydlodd abaty Glyn y Groes ger Llangollen, lle mae wedi’i gladdu.",
   ["Madog ap Gruffudd Maelor was prince of northern Powys for forty-five years, from 1191 until his death in 1236. The land he ruled, from the Dee valley to Maelor, came to be called Powys Fadog after his line. He was the grandson of Owain Gwynedd on his mother’s side and the cousin of Llywelyn Fawr, and he is remembered above all as the founder of the abbey of Glyn y Groes, Valle Crucis, near Llangollen.",
    "|| Father | Gruffudd Maelor, prince of Powys",
    "|| Mother | Angharad, daughter of Owain Gwynedd",
    "|| Prince of northern Powys | 1191–1236",
    "|| Founded | Glyn y Groes (Valle Crucis Abbey), 28 January 1201",
    "|| Wife | Esyllt",
    "|| Died | 1236; buried at Glyn y Groes",
    "## Prince of Powys Fadog",
    "Madog was the elder son of Gruffudd Maelor and of Angharad, the daughter of Owain Gwynedd. From 1191 he ruled northern Powys with his brother Owain, and after Owain’s death in 1197 he ruled it alone. He stood close to his cousin Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, and from 1215 he was Llywelyn’s ally to the end of his days, through the wars that made Llywelyn the leader of all Wales.[[cite:wiki-madog-maelor]]",
    "## Glyn y Groes",
    "On 28 January 1201 Madog founded a house of Cistercian monks in a quiet valley under the hills, a mile and a half north of Llangollen. It took its name, Valle Crucis, the valley of the cross, from the Pillar of Eliseg that stands nearby, the ninth-century cross raised by King Cyngen of Powys in honour of his great-grandfather Elisedd. Madog’s abbey became one of the great houses of Wales, and for three hundred years it was a home of Welsh learning and a patron of the poets. Its church still stands in ruins under the hills, with its great west front and rose window.[[cite:wiki-madog-maelor]]",
    "## Buried among his monks",
    "Madog died in 1236 and was buried in the abbey church at Glyn y Groes, though the exact place of his grave is not known. His sons Gruffudd, Maredudd, Hywel and Madog Fychan came after him, and his grandson Madog ap Gruffudd ruled Powys Fadog in the days of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd. From his line, generations later, came Owain Glyndŵr.[[cite:wiki-madog-maelor]]"],
   ["Bu Madog ap Gruffudd Maelor yn dywysog gogledd Powys am bedwar deg pump o flynyddoedd, o 1191 hyd ei farw yn 1236. Daeth y wlad a reolai, o ddyffryn Dyfrdwy i Faelor, i’w galw’n Bowys Fadog ar ôl ei linach. Roedd yn ŵyr i Owain Gwynedd ar ochr ei fam ac yn gefnder i Lywelyn Fawr, a chofir ef yn anad dim fel sylfaenydd abaty Glyn y Groes ger Llangollen.",
    "|| Tad | Gruffudd Maelor, tywysog Powys",
    "|| Mam | Angharad, merch Owain Gwynedd",
    "|| Tywysog gogledd Powys | 1191–1236",
    "|| Sefydlodd | Glyn y Groes, 28 Ionawr 1201",
    "|| Gwraig | Esyllt",
    "|| Bu farw | 1236; claddwyd yng Nglyn y Groes",
    "## Tywysog Powys Fadog",
    "Madog oedd mab hynaf Gruffudd Maelor ac Angharad, merch Owain Gwynedd. O 1191 rheolodd ogledd Powys gyda’i frawd Owain, ac wedi marw Owain yn 1197 rheolodd ar ei ben ei hun. Safodd yn agos at ei gefnder Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, ac o 1215 bu’n gynghreiriad i Lywelyn hyd ddiwedd ei ddyddiau, drwy’r rhyfeloedd a wnaeth Lywelyn yn arweinydd Cymru gyfan.[[cite:wiki-madog-maelor]]",
    "## Glyn y Groes",
    "Ar 28 Ionawr 1201 sefydlodd Madog dŷ o fynaich Sistersaidd mewn glyn tawel dan y bryniau, filltir a hanner i’r gogledd o Langollen. Cafodd ei enw, Glyn y Groes, Valle Crucis, oddi wrth Biler Eliseg sy’n sefyll gerllaw, y groes o’r nawfed ganrif a gododd y Brenin Cyngen o Bowys er anrhydedd i’w hen daid Elisedd. Daeth abaty Madog yn un o dai mawr Cymru, ac am dri chan mlynedd bu’n gartref i ddysg Gymraeg ac yn noddwr i’r beirdd. Mae ei eglwys yn dal i sefyll yn adfail dan y bryniau, gyda’i thalcen gorllewinol mawr a’i ffenestr rosyn.[[cite:wiki-madog-maelor]]",
    "## Ei gladdu ymhlith ei fynaich",
    "Bu farw Madog yn 1236 a’i gladdu yn eglwys yr abaty yng Nglyn y Groes, er nad yw union fan ei fedd yn hysbys. Daeth ei feibion Gruffudd, Maredudd, Hywel a Madog Fychan ar ei ôl, a rheolodd ei ŵyr Madog ap Gruffudd Bowys Fadog yn nyddiau Llywelyn ap Gruffudd. O’i linach ef, genedlaethau’n ddiweddarach, y daeth Owain Glyndŵr.[[cite:wiki-madog-maelor]]"]),
 P("maelgwn-ap-rhys", "medieval", "c. 1170–1230", "Maelgwn ap Rhys", "Maelgwn ap Rhys", "Prince of Ceredigion, son of the Lord Rhys", "Tywysog Ceredigion, mab yr Arglwydd Rhys", "Maelgwn_ap_Rhys", "rulers",
   "Son of the Lord Rhys, who took the cross with Gerald of Wales in 1188 and ruled Ceredigion for thirty years. He rode with Llywelyn Fawr in the great campaign of 1215 and lies at Strata Florida.",
   "Mab yr Arglwydd Rhys, a gymerodd y groes gyda Gerallt Gymro yn 1188 ac a reolodd Geredigion am ddeng mlynedd ar hugain. Marchogodd gyda Llywelyn Fawr yn ymgyrch fawr 1215 ac mae’n gorwedd yn Ystrad Fflur.",
   ["Maelgwn ap Rhys was a son of the Lord Rhys, the great prince of Deheubarth, and of Gwenllian ferch Madog of Powys. A fighter from his youth, he held Ceredigion for more than thirty years after his father’s death, and he rode with Llywelyn Fawr when the princes of Wales took back the castles of the south in 1215.",
    "|| Born | about 1170",
    "|| Father | Rhys ap Gruffudd, the Lord Rhys",
    "|| Mother | Gwenllian ferch Madog ap Maredudd",
    "|| Lord of Ceredigion | from 1199",
    "|| Died | late in 1230, at Llannerch Aeron",
    "|| Buried | Strata Florida (Ystrad Fflur)",
    "## A son of the Lord Rhys",
    "Maelgwn first appears in 1187, fighting at the siege of Tenby beside his father’s men. In 1188, when Archbishop Baldwin and Gerald of Wales came through Wales to preach the crusade, Maelgwn took the cross. Gerald knew him well, for Gerald was a kinsman of the Lord Rhys. From 1189 to 1194 Maelgwn was held a prisoner, and when his father died in 1197 he came home from exile to claim his share of Deheubarth.[[cite:dwb-maelgwn,wiki-maelgwn]]",
    "## Lord of Ceredigion",
    "From 1199 Maelgwn held Ceredigion, the land between the Dyfi and the Teifi. For thirty years he ruled it through the wars of King John’s reign. In December 1215 he joined Llywelyn ab Iorwerth and his brother Rhys Gryg in the great campaign in which the princes of Wales took the castles of Carmarthen, Kidwelly, Llansteffan, Cardigan and Cilgerran in a single month. At Llywelyn’s council at Aberdyfi in 1216 the lands of Deheubarth were shared among the heirs of the Lord Rhys, and Maelgwn kept Ceredigion south of the Aeron.[[cite:brut-jones,wiki-maelgwn,dwb-maelgwn]]",
    "## Strata Florida",
    "Maelgwn died at Llannerch Aeron late in 1230 and was buried at Strata Florida, the abbey his father had made the burial place of the house of Dinefwr. His son Maelgwn Fychan held Ceredigion after him.[[cite:dwb-maelgwn,wiki-maelgwn]]"],
   ["Mab i’r Arglwydd Rhys, tywysog mawr Deheubarth, ac i Wenllian ferch Madog o Bowys oedd Maelgwn ap Rhys. Yn ymladdwr o’i ieuenctid, daliodd Geredigion am fwy na deng mlynedd ar hugain wedi marw ei dad, a marchogodd gyda Llywelyn Fawr pan gymerodd tywysogion Cymru gestyll y de yn ôl yn 1215.",
    "|| Ganed | tua 1170",
    "|| Tad | Rhys ap Gruffudd, yr Arglwydd Rhys",
    "|| Mam | Gwenllian ferch Madog ap Maredudd",
    "|| Arglwydd Ceredigion | o 1199",
    "|| Bu farw | yn hwyr yn 1230, yn Llannerch Aeron",
    "|| Claddwyd | Ystrad Fflur",
    "## Mab yr Arglwydd Rhys",
    "Ymddengys Maelgwn gyntaf yn 1187, yn ymladd yng ngwarchae Dinbych-y-pysgod gyda gwŷr ei dad. Yn 1188, pan ddaeth yr Archesgob Baldwin a Gerallt Gymro drwy Gymru i bregethu’r groesgad, cymerodd Maelgwn y groes. Adwaenai Gerallt ef yn dda, gan fod Gerallt yn perthyn i’r Arglwydd Rhys. O 1189 hyd 1194 cadwyd Maelgwn yn garcharor, a phan fu farw ei dad yn 1197 daeth adref o alltudiaeth i hawlio ei gyfran o Ddeheubarth.[[cite:dwb-maelgwn,wiki-maelgwn]]",
    "## Arglwydd Ceredigion",
    "O 1199 daliodd Maelgwn Geredigion, y wlad rhwng Dyfi a Theifi. Am ddeng mlynedd ar hugain rheolodd hi drwy ryfeloedd teyrnasiad y Brenin John. Ym mis Rhagfyr 1215 ymunodd â Llywelyn ab Iorwerth a’i frawd Rhys Gryg yn yr ymgyrch fawr pan gymerodd tywysogion Cymru gestyll Caerfyrddin, Cydweli, Llansteffan, Aberteifi a Chilgerran mewn un mis. Yng nghyngor Llywelyn yn Aberdyfi yn 1216 rhannwyd tiroedd Deheubarth rhwng etifeddion yr Arglwydd Rhys, a chadwodd Maelgwn Geredigion i’r de o afon Aeron.[[cite:brut-jones,wiki-maelgwn,dwb-maelgwn]]",
    "## Ystrad Fflur",
    "Bu farw Maelgwn yn Llannerch Aeron yn hwyr yn 1230 a’i gladdu yn Ystrad Fflur, yr abaty a wnaeth ei dad yn fan claddu tŷ Dinefwr. Daliodd ei fab Maelgwn Fychan Geredigion ar ei ôl.[[cite:dwb-maelgwn,wiki-maelgwn]]"]),
 P("rhys-gryg", "medieval", "d. 1234", "Rhys Gryg", "Rhys Gryg", "Prince of Ystrad Tywi, lord of Dinefwr", "Tywysog Ystrad Tywi, arglwydd Dinefwr", "Rhys_Gryg", "rulers",
   "Son of the Lord Rhys, lord of Dinefwr and Ystrad Tywi, who fought beside Llywelyn Fawr from 1215. He fell wounded at Carmarthen in 1234 and lies in St Davids Cathedral.",
   "Mab yr Arglwydd Rhys, arglwydd Dinefwr ac Ystrad Tywi, a ymladdodd ochr yn ochr â Llywelyn Fawr o 1215. Fe’i clwyfwyd yng Nghaerfyrddin yn 1234 ac mae’n gorwedd yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Tyddewi.",
   ["Rhys Gryg, Rhys the Hoarse, also called Rhys Fychan, was the fourth son of the Lord Rhys and of Gwenllian ferch Madog of Powys. He held Dinefwr, the royal seat of Deheubarth, and the lands of Ystrad Tywi, and from 1215 he was one of Llywelyn Fawr’s chief allies in the south. The court poets sang his praise, and he lies in St Davids Cathedral.",
    "|| Father | Rhys ap Gruffudd, the Lord Rhys",
    "|| Mother | Gwenllian ferch Madog ap Maredudd",
    "|| Lord of | Dinefwr, Cantref Mawr and Cantref Bychan, Cydweli and Carnwyllion",
    "|| Wife | Matilda, daughter of Richard de Clare, earl of Hertford",
    "|| Sons | Rhys Mechyll and Maredudd ap Rhys Gryg",
    "|| Died | 1234, at Llandeilo Fawr",
    "|| Buried | St Davids Cathedral",
    "## Lord of Dinefwr",
    "After the Lord Rhys died in 1197, his sons divided Deheubarth among them, and Rhys Gryg made Ystrad Tywi and the rock of Dinefwr his own. He was a hard fighter, and in 1211 and 1212 he and his brother Maelgwn burned the castle King John had built at Aberystwyth.[[cite:wiki-rhys-gryg]]",
    "## With Llywelyn Fawr",
    "From 1215 Rhys stood with Llywelyn ab Iorwerth. In December of that year he rode in the great campaign in which the princes of Wales took Carmarthen, Kidwelly, Llansteffan, Cardigan and Cilgerran. At the council of Aberdyfi in 1216, where Llywelyn shared out the lands of Deheubarth, Rhys was confirmed in the greater part of Cantref Mawr and Cantref Bychan, with Cydweli and Carnwyllion. In 1231 he helped Llywelyn take Cardigan.[[cite:dwb-rhys-gryg,wiki-rhys-gryg,brut-jones]]",
    "The poets of the princes knew him. Prydydd y Moch, the greatest court poet of Gwynedd, sang his praise, and an elegy for him survives, given in the manuscripts to Prydydd y Moch, to Dewi Mynyw or to Prydydd Bychan.[[cite:dwb-rhys-gryg]]",
    "## Carmarthen, 1234",
    "In 1234 Rhys joined his nephew Maelgwn Fychan in an attack on the castle of Carmarthen. He was badly wounded in the fight, and he was carried to Llandeilo Fawr, below Dinefwr, where he died. He was buried in the cathedral of St Davids, where the effigy of a prince in armour has long been held to be his. His son Maredudd ap Rhys Gryg held Dryslwyn after him, and his grandson Rhys ap Maredudd was the last of the house of Dinefwr to rule in Ystrad Tywi.[[cite:dwb-rhys-gryg,wiki-rhys-gryg]]"],
   ["Rhys Gryg, a elwid hefyd Rhys Fychan, oedd pedwerydd mab yr Arglwydd Rhys a Gwenllian ferch Madog o Bowys. Daliodd Ddinefwr, sedd frenhinol Deheubarth, a thiroedd Ystrad Tywi, ac o 1215 roedd yn un o brif gynghreiriaid Llywelyn Fawr yn y de. Canodd beirdd y llys ei glod, ac mae’n gorwedd yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Tyddewi.",
    "|| Tad | Rhys ap Gruffudd, yr Arglwydd Rhys",
    "|| Mam | Gwenllian ferch Madog ap Maredudd",
    "|| Arglwydd | Dinefwr, y Cantref Mawr a’r Cantref Bychan, Cydweli a Charnwyllion",
    "|| Gwraig | Matilda, merch Richard de Clare, iarll Hertford",
    "|| Meibion | Rhys Mechyll a Maredudd ap Rhys Gryg",
    "|| Bu farw | 1234, yn Llandeilo Fawr",
    "|| Claddwyd | Eglwys Gadeiriol Tyddewi",
    "## Arglwydd Dinefwr",
    "Wedi marw’r Arglwydd Rhys yn 1197, rhannodd ei feibion Ddeheubarth rhyngddynt, a gwnaeth Rhys Gryg Ystrad Tywi a chraig Dinefwr yn eiddo iddo’i hun. Roedd yn ymladdwr caled, ac yn 1211 a 1212 llosgodd ef a’i frawd Maelgwn y castell a gododd y Brenin John yn Aberystwyth.[[cite:wiki-rhys-gryg]]",
    "## Gyda Llywelyn Fawr",
    "O 1215 safodd Rhys gyda Llywelyn ab Iorwerth. Ym mis Rhagfyr y flwyddyn honno marchogodd yn yr ymgyrch fawr pan gymerodd tywysogion Cymru Gaerfyrddin, Cydweli, Llansteffan, Aberteifi a Chilgerran. Yng nghyngor Aberdyfi yn 1216, lle rhannodd Llywelyn diroedd Deheubarth, cadarnhawyd Rhys yn y rhan fwyaf o’r Cantref Mawr a’r Cantref Bychan, gyda Chydweli a Charnwyllion. Yn 1231 helpodd Lywelyn i gipio Aberteifi.[[cite:dwb-rhys-gryg,wiki-rhys-gryg,brut-jones]]",
    "Adwaenai beirdd y tywysogion ef. Canodd Prydydd y Moch, bardd llys mwyaf Gwynedd, ei glod, ac erys marwnad iddo, a briodolir yn y llawysgrifau i Brydydd y Moch, i Ddewi Mynyw neu i’r Prydydd Bychan.[[cite:dwb-rhys-gryg]]",
    "## Caerfyrddin, 1234",
    "Yn 1234 ymunodd Rhys â’i nai Maelgwn Fychan mewn ymosodiad ar gastell Caerfyrddin. Clwyfwyd ef yn ddrwg yn yr ymladd, a chariwyd ef i Landeilo Fawr, islaw Dinefwr, lle bu farw. Claddwyd ef yn eglwys gadeiriol Tyddewi, lle credwyd ers tro mai ei ddelw ef yw delw tywysog mewn arfwisg. Daliodd ei fab Maredudd ap Rhys Gryg y Dryslwyn ar ei ôl, a’i ŵyr Rhys ap Maredudd oedd yr olaf o dŷ Dinefwr i reoli yn Ystrad Tywi.[[cite:dwb-rhys-gryg,wiki-rhys-gryg]]"]),
]

